# 6. The numbers we should collect and publish

You specifically asked for tangible numbers: customers buying through WhatsApp, hours spent juggling tools and similar measures.

I agree. But the right numbers are not invented industry statistics. They are measurable facts about the businesses Mupezeni serves.

I would build a small evidence programme around these metrics.

| Metric | How to measure it | Why it matters |
|---|---|---|
| Orders received through WhatsApp per week | Count actual orders over a defined period | Establishes the importance of the channel |
| Percentage of total orders from WhatsApp | WhatsApp orders divided by total orders | Shows channel dependence |
| Average time to process an order | Measure order handling time | Quantifies administrative workload |
| Time spent updating product information | Track time over a representative week | Measures repetitive catalog work |
| Time spent reconciling sales and inventory | Record time spent on the task | Quantifies operational friction |
| Average customer response time | Measure enquiry-to-response time | Shows customer service performance |
| Enquiries converted into orders | Track enquiry and order records | Helps understand sales conversion |
| Stock discrepancies | Compare recorded stock against physical counts | Measures inventory reliability |
| Percentage of supported workflow actions completed successfully | Count verified successful actions | Measures automation reliability |

For every metric, record the measurement period, sample size and definition.

For example:

> Across 12 interviewed retail businesses, the median reported time spent on manual product updates was X hours per week.

We should only publish a statement like that after collecting and analysing the actual data. We should also distinguish reported estimates from measured observations.

### The most valuable first number

I would prioritize:

**"How many hours does a retailer spend each week keeping disconnected systems in sync?"**

It directly expresses the problem Mupezeni is built to solve.

The second would be:

**"How many customer orders does the business receive through WhatsApp each week?"**

It helps establish how important WhatsApp is to the business and provides a useful starting point for measuring the relevant workflow.

Together, these metrics can turn an abstract technology proposition into a concrete business conversation.

